{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over ancient temples. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, the bitterness balanced by the sweetness of wagashi sweets. The silence felt sacred, broken only by the rustle of silk kimonos and the distant chime of temple bells.\n\nBy afternoon, I wandered Nishiki Market, where neon signs glowed over stalls piled high with pickled vegetables, fresh sashimi, and matcha mochi I couldn’t resist buying in bulk. A kind vendor taught me how to properly eat taiyaki—crispy outside, warm red bean inside—while laughing at my clumsy chopstick skills.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya Crossing was a human tide I dared to join, heart pounding as thousands moved in perfect, chaotic sync. I got lost in Akihabara’s electric glow, stumbled upon a robot café, and bought a tiny Hello Kitty keychain I still carry.\n\nOne evening, I took the Shinkansen to Hakone. As the train sliced through mountains, snow-capped Fuji loomed in the distance, perfect and silent. I soaked in an onsen under a twilight sky, steam rising around me, the scent of cedar and sulfur wrapping me in calm.\n\nThe next morning, I visited a tiny shrine in the woods, where I tossed a coin, bowed twice, clapped twice, and whispered a wish I won’t tell anyone. I didn’t need to. The air felt like it already heard me.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—in the rustle of a bamboo curtain, the precision of a folded origami crane, the quiet bow of a shopkeeper. I came for the sights. I left with a stillness I didn’t know I needed.\n\nThree weeks. One suitcase. A heart full of quiet wonder.\n\n—Words by me, under a Kyoto moon.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"to